males and females. The prevalence was 6.2% for males and 6.1% for females. The age was not statistically significant. The prevalence of macular degeneration was 5.2% and the relative risk was 1.538 higher for women than for men. The prevalence of macular degeneration was 4.4% for male and 5.7% for female, which was 1.3% higher for female. macular degeneration. The prevalence of diabetic retinopathy was 2.1%, and the relative risk was not statistically significant for males and females. The prevalence of diabetic retinopathy was 2.5% for males and 1.9% for females, which was 0.6% higher for males. The prevalence of glaucoma was 1.7% and the relative risk was not statistically significant between males and females. The Prevalence of glaucoma was 1.9% for males and 1.7% for females, which was 0.2% higher for males. The age was not statistically significant. The prevalence of ocular hypertension was 0.8%, and th relative risk was 0.533 lower than that of the male. The prevalence of ocular hypertension was 1.2% for males and 0.6% for females, which was 0.6% higher for male. The age was not statistically correlated. There were statistically significant correlations between age-related eye disease and cataract, retinal abnormality, ptosis, visual field defect, glaucoma risk factor, and dry eye. Ophthalmologic diseases with high relative risk for male were high ocular hypertension, ptosis, glaucoma risk factors, and cataracts. Ophthalmologic diseases with high relative risk for female were dry eye and macular degeneration.&#xD;
 The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that cataracts were more common in lung cancer(OR:11.400), gastric cancer(OR:6.846), angina pectoris(OR:6.642), hypertension(OR:6.181), diabetes(OR:5.631), colon cancer(OR:4.768), osteoarthritis(OR:4.546), renal failure (OR:4.451), stroke (OR: 4.435), arthritis(OR:4.318), myocardial infarction(OR:4.238), rheumatoid arthritis (OR: 2.689), asthma(OR:1.839), tuberculosis(OR:1.700), depression(OR:1.493), migraine (OR: 0.680), numbness&amp;migraine(OR:0.566), and atopic dermatitis(OR:0.683) were correlated with 21 systemic diseases.  The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that retinal abnormality were more common in liver cancer(OR:7.625), liver cirrhosis(OR:4.263), diabetes(OR:3.709), colon cancer(OR: 3.017), hypertension(OR: 2.984), hepatitis C(OR: 2.805), osteoarthritis(OR: 2.725), arthritis(OR:2.667), stroke(OR: 2.525), angina pectoris(OR:2.510), rheumatoid arthritis(OR:2.136), dyslipidemia(OR:2.080), myocardial infarction(OR:1.873) tuberculosis(OR:1.369) atopic dermatitis(OR:0.683), migraine(OR:0.649), and numbness&amp;migraine(OR:0.521) were correlated with 17 systemic diseases.). The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that visual field defect were more common in gastric cancer(OR:3.090), stroke(OR:2.935), diabetes(OR:2.705), cervical cancer(OR:2.664), angina pectoris(OR:2.642), hypertension(OR:1.429), asthma(OR:1.429), dyslipidemia(OR:1.426), migraine(OR: 0.756) were correlated with 14 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that ptosis were more common in angina pectoris(OR:3.322), diabetes(OR:3.165), hypertension(OR:3.144), stroke(OR: 3.041), myocardial infarction(OR:2.454), arthritis(OR:2.363), osteoarthritis(OR:2.363), rheumatoid arthritis(OR:2.134), dyslipidemia(OR:1.998), asthma(OR:1.570), numbness(OR0.794), migraine(OR:0.693), and numbness&amp;migraine were correlated with 13 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that dry eye were more common in renal failure(OR:3.358), thyroid cancer(OR:3.087), numbness&amp;migraine(OR:2.442), thyroid disease(OR:2.386), rheumatoid arthritis(OR:2.252), arthritis(OR:1.979), osteoarthritis(OR:1.905), dyslipidemia(OR:1.894), depression(OR1.825), migraine(OR:1.507) numbness(OR:1.481), and hypertension(OR:1.238) were correlated with 12 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that visual field defect were more common in hepatits (OR:4.527), cervical cancer(OR:3.150), myocardial infarction(OR:2.932), stroke(OR:2.761), colon cancer(OR:2.703), hypertension(OR:2.434), angina pectoris(OR:2.135), diabetes(OR:2.057), dyslipidemia(OR:1.645), arthritis(OR:1.387), osteoarthritis(OR:1.387), and numbness&amp;migraine(OR:0.580) were correlated with 12 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that macular degeneration were more common liver cancer(OR:56.067), liver cirrhosis (OR: 9.502), hypertension(OR:3.519), arthritis(OR:3.318), osteoarthritis(OR:3.318), breast cancer(OR:3.055), rheumatoid arthritis(OR:2.651), Hepatitis B(OR:2.393), stroke(OR: 2.118), diabetes(OR:2.096) and dyslipidemia(OR:1.710) were correlated with 11 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that diabetic retinopathy were more common in diabetes(OR:140.663), angina pectoris(OR:7.320), liver cirrhosis(OR:6.902), stroke(OR:6.495), renal failure (OR:6.284), dyslipidemia(OR:4.807), myocardial infarction(OR4.045), hypertension(OR: 3.986), rheumatoid arthritis(OR:3.734), and migraine(OR:0.369). were correlated with 10 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that ocular hypertension were more common in tuberculosis(OR:3.470) and diabetes(OR:2.988) were correlated with 2 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that hypotony were more common in liver cancer(OR:7.948) and migraine(OR:0.663) were correlated with 2 systemic diseases. The cross-sectional analysis of systemic diseases and eye disease showed that glaucoma were more common in diabetes(OR:2.395) and hypertension(OR:2.050). were correlated with 2 systemic diseases. The higher the prevalence of eye disease, the greater the number of correlated diseases associated with systemic disease.&#xD;
  In this study, we presented meaningful results that conducted a comprehensive analysis of systemic diseases and ocular diseases in koreans in order to investigate the relative risk, prevalence, gender, and age-related relationships between systemic diseases and ocular diseases. In some cases, eye disease alone may be present. However, in many cases, the systematic disease is related to the invention of eye disease.  Therefore ,the association results from this study can be used as basic data for the cause of ocular disease. The results of this study suggest that the order of correlations with the degree of correlation with the eye diseases and the degree of correlation are very useful for the prediction of the disease as well as the treatment.&#xD;
